1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. Ng5 d5 5. exd5 Nxd5 $2 {Don't all the Fried
Liver :)} (5... Na5 $1 6. Bb5+ c6 7. dxc6 bxc6 {This main line would be an even
better antidote in this case because Black is already up a rook so sacrificing a
pawn for activity particularly suits him and reduces Paul's chances to
complicate the game.}) 6. Nxf7 $5 {This is very dangerous and strong...} (6. d4 $1
{...but this is even better. I learned this in a correspondence game where I
couldn't force Black's defenses after 6.Nxf7 $5 though my opponent did err
eventually. In practice, both options are strong.}) 6... Kxf7 7. Qf3+ Ke6 {The
only way to evade check is to advance with the king.} (7... Kg8 $4 8. Qxd5+ Qxd5
9. Bxd5+ Be6 10. Bxe6# {is a painful mate that has befallen many amateurs. I had
one student (a 1200 adult) who allowed this repeatedly $1}) 8. Nc3 Nd4 $2 {Simply
defending makes sense. I don't know why Black surrendered d5, but presumably
Black thought Nxc2+ had merit.} (8... Nb4 $1) (8... Ne7 $1) 9. Bxd5+ Kd6 10. Qf7 $1
{Ne4# is threatened.} 10... Be6 $2 {This LOOKS good, but it is in fact nearly
losing after Morphy's excellent sequence.} (10... Nxc2+ $2 11. Kd1 {Black still
has to cover Ne4# AND the knight on c2 hangs.}) (10... Qe7 {This is the simplest
response. After Ne4+ there is ...Kd7, and of course, White cannot trade queens
due to the missing rook on a1.}) 11. Bxe6 Nxe6 12. Ne4+ Kd5 (12... Kc6 {giving
up the knight (and suffering) is needed.}) 13. c4+ $1 {Once the king is forced in
front of his pawns, a proper king hunt often offers more material to drag him
deeper into the swamp.} 13... Kxe4 14. Qxe6 Qd4 {There is nothing better really.
All defenses lose.} (14... Kd3 {Stockfish's preferred try.} 15. Qg4 $1 {and the
Black king is still trapped.}) 15. Qg4+ Kd3 16. Qe2+ Kc2 17. d3+ $1 {A pleasing
discovery which sets up the beautiful mate.} 17... Kxc1 (17... Kb1 {Obviously
this is more resistant, but White still forces mate.} 18. O-O Kxa2 19. Qc2 b5
20. b3+ Ka1 21. Bh6#) 18. O-O# {Checkmate by castling $1 A rare and special
opportunity $1} 1-0

